[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed PostgreSQL Architectures with Citus"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As data volumes grow beyond the limits of a single PostgreSQL node, scaling up becomes cost-prohibitive. Citus transforms standard PostgreSQL into a distributed database, allowing you to horizontally scale tables across multiple machines (nodes) while maintaining full SQL compatibility."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Citus Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Citus cluster consists of a coordinator node and multiple worker nodes. The coordinator node routes incoming SQL queries to the appropriate worker nodes, which store the actual data shards. Queries are executed in parallel across the workers, drastically reducing latency for large-scale analytical and transactional workloads."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Installation"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before using Citus, you must install the extension on all nodes in your PostgreSQL cluster. Assuming PostgreSQL is already installed, add the Citus repository and install the package using your system package manager."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"sudo apt-get install -y postgresql-15-citus-11.2"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, configure your postgresql.conf file to load the citus shared library on startup."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"shared_preload_libraries = 'citus'"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Enabling the Citus Extension"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once PostgreSQL is restarted with the shared library loaded, connect to your database and create the Citus extension."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"CREATE EXTENSION citus;"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding Worker Nodes"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To form the cluster, you must register your worker nodes with the coordinator node using the master_add_node function."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"SELECT master_add_node('worker-1-ip', 5432);
SELECT master_add_node('worker-2-ip', 5432);"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Distributed Tables and Sharding"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike standard tables, distributed tables in Citus are split into smaller pieces called shards. You choose a distribution column (shard key) based on your access patterns."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE events (
    event_id bigint,
    user_id bigint,
    event_date timestamp,
    payload jsonb
);

SELECT create_distributed_table('events', 'user_id');"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Distributed Workloads"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Choose a distribution column that is frequently used in your WHERE clauses and JOIN conditions."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Co-locate tables that are frequently joined by using the same distribution column."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use reference tables for small, infrequently updated lookup tables that need to be joined with distributed tables."}]
